{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792370195692,"sessionID":"ses_eae696a14ffehCxB980snPYjCy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_mz57tj3zp7cq0pkh6f80y67q","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\93rob\\Documents\\GitHub\\SpaceFace\\build_map.md","offset":300,"limit":100},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\93rob\\Documents\\GitHub\\SpaceFace\\build_map.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n300: | \"the ships / objects look like toys\" | **§13D** → ships: `PQ-050` (Forge, `tools/blender/forge/FORGE.md`); places: `--id PQ-193`; unused packs stay `PQ-136` |\n301: | \"NPC ships look like another game / floating parts / reverse jets are needles\" | **§13D** → `--id PQ-193` leaves `.01` / `.02`. Law: [`design/program/VISUAL_WORLD_CLEANUP.md`](./design/program/VISUAL_WORLD_CLEANUP.md). Hitch stays frozen. |\n302: | \"ships don't render / pieces missing / empty targeting lock\" | **§13D** → `--id PQ-193` leaf `.00`. Complete packaged body, not a procedural fallback. Do not unhide a box while waiting. Process: [`design/program/DYNAMIC_GRAPHICS_INVESTIGATION.md`](./design/program/DYNAMIC_GRAPHICS_INVESTIGATION.md). |\n303: | \"the world feels dead / nobody reacts\" | §13C `PQ-138`, then §15 `PQ-149`–`PQ-151` |\n304: | \"I can't tell what anything is\" | `PQ-161` readable at zoom, `PQ-153` sectors, `PQ-162` station |\n305: | \"the sound is bad / there's no music\" | `PQ-158` (after the ALPHA gate) |\n306: | \"it crashed / my save is gone / it won't start\" | integrity first: §7, `PQ-033`, the save checks |\n307: \n308: Do not sweep `design/`, `.campaign/`, assets, transcripts, or screenshots for an ordinary unit.\n309: \n310: ### 1.8 Production maturity: debugging and polish\n311: \n312: **Owner direction, 2026-10-04:** mature the existing game into a dependable, polished experience.\n313: Feature expansion alone is not progress when ordinary play is buggy. Agents own finding,\n314: reproducing and fixing failures; the owner does not need jargon or a bug-by-bug briefing.\n315: This is an active production priority across §27's existing lanes, not a second queue or an audit\n316: deliverable. The ONE open-defect ledger remains\n317: [`DEMO_READINESS_2026-09-20.md` §6](./design/program/DEMO_READINESS_2026-09-20.md#6-the-demo-defect-ledger--everything-a-demo-player-will-hit-live-opened-2026-09-22).\n318: \n319: **Copy-ready assignment:** \"Debug and polish SpaceFace using build_map §1.8. Own discovery\n320: and repair of the assigned area; for a whole-game campaign cover every workflow in the table.\n321: Fix production causes, verify ordinary play and recovery, and report what now works and any\n322: remaining defects in plain player language. You own finding the bugs, not just checking a list.\"\n323: \n324: **Choose repairs before expansion.** First: crashes, lost progress, failed launch/Continue,\n325: softlocks and unusable controls. Then: repeatable hitches, missing bodies, broken transactions,\n326: unreadable or unreachable UI and incorrect gameplay feedback. Then: visual, audio and interaction\n327: polish. Rank by severity, frequency and reach, not by how easy the task is to count. Fix an\n328: available defect in the selected area before extending it; unaffected work can continue in disjoint\n329: paths. `done` on a feature or `free` on a seam says nothing about current product quality.\n330: \n331: **Discover by player workflow.** A maturity assignment covers an ordinary workflow and its\n332: failure/recovery edges, not just the happy path or a named function. Use the following coverage\n333: to select area-sized repair work. Across a finishing campaign, cover every row; a single fix\n334: checks only the affected row and adjacent transitions. Reuse current results when the relevant\n335: owners have not changed. These are assignments and completion bars, not new status IDs.\n336: \n337: | Player problem / coverage | Existing owner and concrete repair assignment | The result the player should get |\n338: |---|---|---|\n339: | **Start, stop and resume** | THE MACHINE + THE LONG GAME: cold New Game → Launch → control; save → title/restart → Continue; death → retry; failed/invalid load → recover. Cover Browser and Electron's shared route and any affected shell boundary. | No crash, endless spinner, invisible world, lost progress, duplicate rewards or dead end. A failed operation explains itself and leaves a usable route back. |\n340: | **Controls and everyday play** | THE HAND + THE FIGHT: ordinary flight, targeting, mining, towing, fighting and camera/zoom; pause/settings/modal → resume; held/released inputs, rebinding and focus changes. | Input answers once, the camera and body agree, and closing a screen never leaves stuck controls, accidental actions or an unusable ship. |\n341: | **Menus, HUD and text** | The workflow owner repairs functional UI within ORRERY: exercise title/new game/load/settings/pause, map, station/market/outfitting, jobs and Swarm draft/refit/results. Check narrow/default/wide layouts, long names/numbers, +40% pseudo-localized copy, populated/empty/error states, scrolling and focus. | No overflow, overlap, cropped meaning, off-screen action, unreadable feedback or dead control. Needed information and controls remain reachable by the supported input methods; motion/accessibility settings preserve meaning. |\n342: | **The visible world loads and stays there** | THE PICTURE + THE MACHINE: camera approach/turn/zoom, fast travel, sector arrival/exit, combat spawns and save/load. Repair the actual asset resolution, fetch/decode/compose/compile/admission/residency owner that fails. | Ships, stations, rocks, wrecks and effects appear as authored bodies when needed; nothing usable is an empty lock, invisible collider, missing part or disappearing on-screen model. Loading failure is recoverable and cannot silently count as readiness. |\n343: | **Off-screen work and loading pressure** | THE MACHINE with THE PICTURE/WORLD: bound predictive admission, decode/compile queues, draw submission, distant queries, texture/model residency and retirement. Check rapid direction reversals, arrivals and repeated sector revisits. Separate render cost from simulation authority. | Nearby and imminent content wins priority; distant content does not flood expensive queues or remain resident without need. Culling/residency does not pop visible bodies, discard imminent arrivals, erase NPC consequences or change default visual quality. |\n344: | **Hitches and long-session stability** | THE MACHINE: repair identified main-thread stalls, allocation/GC churn, repeated setup, unbounded scans, resource/event/timer leaks and stale async callbacks through dock/undock/jump/fight/save/load cycles. | Stable frame pacing and bounded resources over ordinary transitions. Use existing demo/min-spec budgets; report frame tails and missing-body evidence as well as averages. A smoother empty scene does not pass. |\n345: | **Transactions, jobs and progression** | THE WORLD + THE LONG GAME: accept → do → settle/abandon/fail → reload; trade, buy/fit/use, cargo custody, credits, law and unlocks. Include invalid/stale selections and repeated clicks. | Displayed price, eligibility, inventory, physical result and saved result agree. Settlement happens once; cancellation/failure leaves coherent play and useful feedback. |\n346: | **Visual and audio finish** | THE PICTURE + THE EAR + the affected workflow owner: broken transforms/materials/lighting, clipping/z-fighting, stuck/floating effects, unreadable cues, missing/duplicated sound and effects surviving their owner. | The authored picture and feedback remain coherent at the gameplay camera, dense moments and supported accessibility settings. Repair defects within the existing art direction. |\n347: \n348: **Run a finite find → fix → verify loop.**\n349: \n350: 1. Pick the highest-impact available ledger defect, or one workflow above lacking current\n351:    coverage. Check the live owner and exact dirty paths. State the player-visible outcome in\n352:    one sentence. A closed packet is a lead, not a reason to dismiss a current failure.\n353: 2. Exercise the real workflow with representative existing content. Include the relevant\n354:    interruption, repeated action or recovery edge. Existing fixtures, DOM/layout checks and\n355:    fixed-seed routes are the default; do not create a universal crawler or another census.\n356:    A full campaign rotates through the table rather than repeating the same easy opening.\n357: 3. Reproduce a found failure at the cheapest relevant layer and fix the production cause.\n358:    If a missing lifecycle owner, loading policy, cancellation path, resource budget or shared\n359:    layout primitive is necessary, build that specific structure and wire it into the default\n360:    route. Prefer that shared repair over scattered symptom patches; no speculative rewrite.\n361: 4. Verify the affected workflow and its failure/recovery edge after the fix. Add a focused\n362:    regression when recurrence or a meaningful boundary warrants it; do not write tests that\n363:    merely mirror the code. Run affected existing checks once; broaden only for a real failure\n364:    or a change that crosses more owners. Green tests cannot prove a visual or timing claim\n365:    they do not observe.\n366: 5. Commit the owned repair by exact paths, removing its ledger row in the fixing commit.\n367:    Out-of-area defects follow root `AGENTS.md` §7: fix small ones, delegate medium ones,\n368:    otherwise one ledger row with what the player sees, the route/actions, expected/actual\n369:    behavior, frequency/seed if known and the cheapest repro. Never substitute a report for\n370:    an assigned repair or silently discard a defect because its cause is unknown.\n371: 6. A bounded assignment ends when its outcome works, direct verification passes and no\n372:    unresolved defect materially changes that outcome. A broader maturity campaign continues\n373:    through uncovered workflows and remaining repairs until its agreed scope is complete.\n374:    Report coverage and any material remaining defect plainly; do not call a whole game\n375:    flawless because one fixture or one route passed. No repeated confidence-only review.\n376: \n377: **Demo-ready is a product condition, separate from publishing.** The existing demo path\n378: (§21/§25: launch, fight, results/retry, Adventure job, purchase/fit/use an upgrade, save/Continue)\n379: must work with the real picture and reachable UI, without known crashes, lost progress, softlocks,\n380: visible-body failures, obstructed controls/text or repeatable player-disrupting hitches in that\n381: scope. The table above covers ordinary play beyond that showcase; a narrow demo pass does not\n382: close the whole maturity program. Packaging/store work keeps its existing release authorization.\n383: Maturity fixes remain active while THE RELEASE is parked.\n384: \n385: **Use the right host without stalling repairs.** Clean-machine captures, absolute performance\n386: readings and soaks belong only to [`QUIET_HOST_PROGRAM.md`](./design/program/QUIET_HOST_PROGRAM.md).\n387: On the shared dev box, fix known causes with source inspection and focused functional/layout/\n388: resource checks; do not produce captures, hunt historical freezes, or claim a hardware speedup\n389: from contended timings. Consume the quiet-host receipt for the broader timing/visual verdict\n390: and route real failures back to these repair owners. Human play or approval is not a repair gate.\n391: \n392: ### 1.9 Agentic production — short learning loops, complete outcomes\n393: \n394: **Owner direction, 2026-10-11:** development is entirely agentic. Consolidate work around useful\n395: player outcomes; spend iteration time improving the game. This section supersedes older routine\n396: capture, human-playtest, multi-reviewer and full-boot prescriptions. It preserves technical\n397: contracts, direct verification and meaningful visual inspection. External publishing keeps its\n398: existing authorization; a human gameplay opinion is never an execution gate.\n399: \n\n(Showing lines 300-399 of 5455. Use offset=400 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"| \"the ships / objects look like toys\" | **§13D** → ships: `PQ-050` (Forge, `tools/blender/forge/FORGE.md`); places: `--id PQ-193`; unused packs stay `PQ-136` |\n| \"NPC ships look like another game / floating parts / reverse jets are needles\" | **§13D** → `--id PQ-193` leaves `.01` / `.02`. Law: [`design/program/VISUAL_WORLD_CLEANUP.md`](./design/program/VISUAL_WORLD_CLEANUP.md). Hitch stays frozen. |\n| \"ships don't render / pieces missing / empty targeting lock\" | **§13D** → `--id PQ-193` leaf `.00`. Complete packaged body, not a procedural fallback. Do not unhide a box while waiting. Process: [`design/program/DYNAMIC_GRAPHICS_INVESTIGATION.md`](./design/program/DYNAMIC_GRAPHICS_INVESTIGATION.md). |\n| \"the world feels dead / nobody reacts\" | §13C `PQ-138`, then §15 `PQ-149`–`PQ-151` |\n| \"I can't tell what anything is\" | `PQ-161` readable at zoom, `PQ-153` sectors, `PQ-162` station |\n| \"the sound is bad / there's no music\" | `PQ-158` (after the ALPHA gate) |\n| \"it crashed / my save is gone / it won't start\" | integrity first: §7, `PQ-033`, the save checks |\n\nDo not sweep `design/`, `.campaign/`, assets, transcripts, or screenshots for an ordinary unit.\n\n### 1.8 Production maturity: debugging and polish\n\n**Owner direction, 2026-10-04:** mature the existing game into a dependable, polished experience.\nFeature expansion alone is not progress when ordinary play is buggy. Agents own finding,\nreproducing and fixing failures; the owner does not need jargon or a bug-by-bug briefing.\nThis is an active production priority across §27's existing lanes, not a second queue or an audit\ndeliverable. The ONE open-defect ledger remains\n[`DEMO_READINESS_2026-09-20.md` §6](./design/program/DEMO_READINESS_2026-09-20.md#6-the-demo-defect-ledger--everything-a-demo-player-will-hit-live-opened-2026-09-22).\n\n**Copy-ready assignment:** \"Debug and polish SpaceFace using build_map §1.8. Own discovery","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\93rob\\Documents\\GitHub\\SpaceFace\\build_map.md","text":"| \"the ships / objects look like toys\" | **§13D** → ships: `PQ-050` (Forge, `tools/blender/forge/FORGE.md`); places: `--id PQ-193`; unused packs stay `PQ-136` |\n| \"NPC ships look like another game / floating parts / reverse jets are needles\" | **§13D** → `--id PQ-193` leaves `.01` / `.02`. Law: [`design/program/VISUAL_WORLD_CLEANUP.md`](./design/program/VISUAL_WORLD_CLEANUP.md). Hitch stays frozen. |\n| \"ships don't render / pieces missing / empty targeting lock\" | **§13D** → `--id PQ-193` leaf `.00`. Complete packaged body, not a procedural fallback. Do not unhide a box while waiting. Process: [`design/program/DYNAMIC_GRAPHICS_INVESTIGATION.md`](./design/program/DYNAMIC_GRAPHICS_INVESTIGATION.md). |\n| \"the world feels dead / nobody reacts\" | §13C `PQ-138`, then §15 `PQ-149`–`PQ-151` |\n| \"I can't tell what anything is\" | `PQ-161` readable at zoom, `PQ-153` sectors, `PQ-162` station |\n| \"the sound is bad / there's no music\" | `PQ-158` (after the ALPHA gate) |\n| \"it crashed / my save is gone / it won't start\" | integrity first: §7, `PQ-033`, the save checks |\n\nDo not sweep `design/`, `.campaign/`, assets, transcripts, or screenshots for an ordinary unit.\n\n### 1.8 Production maturity: debugging and polish\n\n**Owner direction, 2026-10-04:** mature the existing game into a dependable, polished experience.\nFeature expansion alone is not progress when ordinary play is buggy. Agents own finding,\nreproducing and fixing failures; the owner does not need jargon or a bug-by-bug briefing.\nThis is an active production priority across §27's existing lanes, not a second queue or an audit\ndeliverable. The ONE open-defect ledger remains\n[`DEMO_READINESS_2026-09-20.md` §6](./design/program/DEMO_READINESS_2026-09-20.md#6-the-demo-defect-ledger--everything-a-demo-player-will-hit-live-opened-2026-09-22).\n\n**Copy-ready assignment:** \"Debug and polish SpaceFace using build_map §1.8. Own discovery\nand repair of the assigned area; for a whole-game campaign cover every workflow in the table.\nFix production causes, verify ordinary play and recovery, and report what now works and any\nremaining defects in plain player language. You own finding the bugs, not just checking a list.\"\n\n**Choose repairs before expansion.** First: crashes, lost progress, failed launch/Continue,\nsoftlocks and unusable controls. Then: repeatable hitches, missing bodies, broken transactions,\nunreadable or unreachable UI and incorrect gameplay feedback. Then: visual, audio and interaction\npolish. Rank by severity, frequency and reach, not by how easy the task is to count. Fix an\navailable defect in the selected area before extending it; unaffected work can continue in disjoint\npaths. `done` on a feature or `free` on a seam says nothing about current product quality.\n\n**Discover by player workflow.** A maturity assignment covers an ordinary workflow and its\nfailure/recovery edges, not just the happy path or a named function. Use the following coverage\nto select area-sized repair work. Across a finishing campaign, cover every row; a single fix\nchecks only the affected row and adjacent transitions. Reuse current results when the relevant\nowners have not changed. These are assignments and completion bars, not new status IDs.\n\n| Player problem / coverage | Existing owner and concrete repair assignment | The result the player should get |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Start, stop and resume** | THE MACHINE + THE LONG GAME: cold New Game → Launch → control; save → title/restart → Continue; death → retry; failed/invalid load → recover. Cover Browser and Electron's shared route and any affected shell boundary. | No crash, endless spinner, invisible world, lost progress, duplicate rewards or dead end. A failed operation explains itself and leaves a usable route back. |\n| **Controls and everyday play** | THE HAND + THE FIGHT: ordinary flight, targeting, mining, towing, fighting and camera/zoom; pause/settings/modal → resume; held/released inputs, rebinding and focus changes. | Input answers once, the camera and body agree, and closing a screen never leaves stuck controls, accidental actions or an unusable ship. |\n| **Menus, HUD and text** | The workflow owner repairs functional UI within ORRERY: exercise title/new game/load/settings/pause, map, station/market/outfitting, jobs and Swarm draft/refit/results. Check narrow/default/wide layouts, long names/numbers, +40% pseudo-localized copy, populated/empty/error states, scrolling and focus. | No overflow, overlap, cropped meaning, off-screen action, unreadable feedback or dead control. Needed information and controls remain reachable by the supported input methods; motion/accessibility settings preserve meaning. |\n| **The visible world loads and stays there** | THE PICTURE + THE MACHINE: camera approach/turn/zoom, fast travel, sector arrival/exit, combat spawns and save/load. Repair the actual asset resolution, fetch/decode/compose/compile/admission/residency owner that fails. | Ships, stations, rocks, wrecks and effects appear as authored bodies when needed; nothing usable is an empty lock, invisible collider, missing part or disappearing on-screen model. Loading failure is recoverable and cannot silently count as readiness. |\n| **Off-screen work and loading pressure** | THE MACHINE with THE PICTURE/WORLD: bound predictive admission, decode/compile queues, draw submission, distant queries, texture/model residency and retirement. Check rapid direction reversals, arrivals and repeated sector revisits. Separate render cost from simulation authority. | Nearby and imminent content wins priority; distant content does not flood expensive queues or remain resident without need. Culling/residency does not pop visible bodies, discard imminent arrivals, erase NPC consequences or change default visual quality. |\n| **Hitches and long-session stability** | THE MACHINE: repair identified main-thread stalls, allocation/GC churn, repeated setup, unbounded scans, resource/event/timer leaks and stale async callbacks through dock/undock/jump/fight/save/load cycles. | Stable frame pacing and bounded resources over ordinary transitions. Use existing demo/min-spec budgets; report frame tails and missing-body evidence as well as averages. A smoother empty scene does not pass. |\n| **Transactions, jobs and progression** | THE WORLD + THE LONG GAME: accept → do → settle/abandon/fail → reload; trade, buy/fit/use, cargo custody, credits, law and unlocks. Include invalid/stale selections and repeated clicks. | Displayed price, eligibility, inventory, physical result and saved result agree. Settlement happens once; cancellation/failure leaves coherent play and useful feedback. |\n| **Visual and audio finish** | THE PICTURE + THE EAR + the affected workflow owner: broken transforms/materials/lighting, clipping/z-fighting, stuck/floating effects, unreadable cues, missing/duplicated sound and effects surviving their owner. | The authored picture and feedback remain coherent at the gameplay camera, dense moments and supported accessibility settings. Repair defects within the existing art direction. |\n\n**Run a finite find → fix → verify loop.**\n\n1. Pick the highest-impact available ledger defect, or one workflow above lacking current\n   coverage. Check the live owner and exact dirty paths. State the player-visible outcome in\n   one sentence. A closed packet is a lead, not a reason to dismiss a current failure.\n2. Exercise the real workflow with representative existing content. Include the relevant\n   interruption, repeated action or recovery edge. Existing fixtures, DOM/layout checks and\n   fixed-seed routes are the default; do not create a universal crawler or another census.\n   A full campaign rotates through the table rather than repeating the same easy opening.\n3. Reproduce a found failure at the cheapest relevant layer and fix the production cause.\n   If a missing lifecycle owner, loading policy, cancellation path, resource budget or shared\n   layout primitive is necessary, build that specific structure and wire it into the default\n   route. Prefer that shared repair over scattered symptom patches; no speculative rewrite.\n4. Verify the affected workflow and its failure/recovery edge after the fix. Add a focused\n   regression when recurrence or a meaningful boundary warrants it; do not write tests that\n   merely mirror the code. Run affected existing checks once; broaden only for a real failure\n   or a change that crosses more owners. Green tests cannot prove a visual or timing claim\n   they do not observe.\n5. Commit the owned repair by exact paths, removing its ledger row in the fixing commit.\n   Out-of-area defects follow root `AGENTS.md` §7: fix small ones, delegate medium ones,\n   otherwise one ledger row with what the player sees, the route/actions, expected/actual\n   behavior, frequency/seed if known and the cheapest repro. Never substitute a report for\n   an assigned repair or silently discard a defect because its cause is unknown.\n6. A bounded assignment ends when its outcome works, direct verification passes and no\n   unresolved defect materially changes that outcome. A broader maturity campaign continues\n   through uncovered workflows and remaining repairs until its agreed scope is complete.\n   Report coverage and any material remaining defect plainly; do not call a whole game\n   flawless because one fixture or one route passed. No repeated confidence-only review.\n\n**Demo-ready is a product condition, separate from publishing.** The existing demo path\n(§21/§25: launch, fight, results/retry, Adventure job, purchase/fit/use an upgrade, save/Continue)\nmust work with the real picture and reachable UI, without known crashes, lost progress, softlocks,\nvisible-body failures, obstructed controls/text or repeatable player-disrupting hitches in that\nscope. The table above covers ordinary play beyond that showcase; a narrow demo pass does not\nclose the whole maturity program. Packaging/store work keeps its existing release authorization.\nMaturity fixes remain active while THE RELEASE is parked.\n\n**Use the right host without stalling repairs.** Clean-machine captures, absolute performance\nreadings and soaks belong only to [`QUIET_HOST_PROGRAM.md`](./design/program/QUIET_HOST_PROGRAM.md).\nOn the shared dev box, fix known causes with source inspection and focused functional/layout/\nresource checks; do not produce captures, hunt historical freezes, or claim a hardware speedup\nfrom contended timings. Consume the quiet-host receipt for the broader timing/visual verdict\nand route real failures back to these repair owners. Human play or approval is not a repair gate.\n\n### 1.9 Agentic production — short learning loops, complete outcomes\n\n**Owner direction, 2026-10-11:** development is entirely agentic. Consolidate work around useful\nplayer outcomes; spend iteration time improving the game. This section supersedes older routine\ncapture, human-playtest, multi-reviewer and full-boot prescriptions. It preserves technical\ncontracts, direct verification and meaningful visual inspection. External publishing keeps its\nexisting authorization; a human gameplay opinion is never an execution gate.\n","lineStart":300,"lineEnd":399,"totalLines":5455,"truncated":true}},"title":"build_map.md","time":{"start":1792370194955,"end":1792370195672}},"id":"prt_15196b938001dL56HBKLPVYKrn","sessionID":"ses_eae696a14ffehCxB980snPYjCy","messageID":"msg_151969acf001K4eJ4wORqCi9q9"}}
